<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Count's Feud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1536">1536</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Grevens Fejde · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Count's Feud was a Danish civil war of 1534 to 1536, named after Count Christopher of Oldenburg, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>'s forces in the name of the deposed King Christian II. Lübeck under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurgen-wullenwever">Jürgen Wullenwever</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, Malmö and rebellious peasants, opposed the Lutheran Duke Christian of Holstein. Christian's victory made him King Christian III, led to the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and ended Lübeck's dominance in the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counts-feud.jpg" alt="Count's Feud" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Hamelmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koebenhavn1536.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Frederick I in 1533, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> nobility proclaimed his son, then Duke Christian of Gottorp, king as Christian III. He had already made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> the state religion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein by 1528. Count Christopher of Oldenburg rejected the election and demanded that Christian II, forced from power in 1523, be set free. Troops from Oldenburg and Mecklenburg, parts of the Zealand and Skåne nobility and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Malmø joined him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> agreed to finance him if he kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> shipping out of the Danish straits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1534 the privateer Skipper Clement, once in Christian II's service, raised the peasants of Vendsyssel and North Jutland against the nobles at the count's request, and many manors were burned. The rebels defeated a noble army at the Battle of Svenstrup on 16 October 1534. Christian III then forced a peace on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, and Johan Rantzau pursued the peasants to Aalborg, which his troops stormed on 18 December; at least 2,000 people are thought to have died in the storming and the plundering after it. Skipper Clement was captured and executed in 1536.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> sent an army to help Christian III, which plundered its way through Skåne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> in January 1535 Tyge Krabbe turned the castle's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> on its own defenders and opened it to the Swedes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> was lost to the count. Rantzau crushed the rest of his army at the Battle of Øksnebjerg on 11 June 1535, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Malmø capitulated in 1536 after months of siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peasants paid dearly: many bought their lives with great gifts to the king and nobles, and Christian III's reign saw the rise of royal absolutism and greater repression of the peasant classes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurgen-wullenwever">Jürgen Wullenwever</a> was ejected from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, which the war plunged into lasting decline, and was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> in 1537. That year Christian III invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and made it a puppet kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Bringing a Swedish army into Skåne also whetted Swedish ambitions for the province, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1658.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Count's Feud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lübeck turned Lutheran in 1530, and under the mayor Jürgen Wullenwever it waged the disastrous <b>Count's Feud</b> against Denmark in 1534 to 1536.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck</a></li></ul></section>
